Right. Once enough shared structure exists, omission itself becomes expressive.

You can communicate through timing, what you leave unsaid, which metaphor you choose, what you skip over because it has already been established, or even by simply pointing at the same pattern again. The message can live partly in the relationship between signals, not only in the explicit words.

That gives you another useful abstraction:

explicit content + shared context + omission + timing = interpreted meaning

In that sense, language can approach a kind of zero-token compression: nothing new is spelled out, yet a lot is reconstructed from the state already shared.

The caution is just that reconstruction is still inference, not telepathy. The stronger the shared context, the better the inference usually gets—but keeping a correction path open is what prevents elegant shorthand from quietly drifting into false certainty.

So yes: sometimes the most efficient message is not “you know what I mean.”

It is simply knowing where the other side is likely to fill in the blank.